WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.250
Yeah so neodymium is one my favourite f elements
to work with. When

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:09.969
you use it in compounds and you dissolve it,
often you get colourful blue

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:13.750
solutions from it. Sometimes they are green
as well, but often I have

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.310
found that if concentration of the solution
makes a really big difference

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.550
to the colour of these solutions so you may
have a compound which

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.230
appears to be red but actually as you dilute
it down eventually it ends up

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.830
being blue, which is really pretty and sometimes
if you cannula it from

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.130
one flask to another and you can see through
the cannula you can see it

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.870
change colour as it goes through the cannula
and then drops into the

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.609
next solution again.

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:41.449
So neodymium is one of the rare earths and
it has a number of quite

00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:47.840
important applications. One of my favourite
is in lasers, it’s used for

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.329
example in this laser pointer and if you point
the laser it makes this

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:56.140
rather nice bright green colour.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.889
A little known use of neodymium is that the
size and strength of volcanic

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:05.000
eruptions can actually be judged from the
composition of neodymium

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.960
isotopes which are present.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.890
The first laser pointers were red. They didn’t
use neodymium, and the

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:16.000
green ones have become really quite fashionable
among lecturers. So

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.310
the way this works is that inside here there
is a small crystal, of so-called

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.700
neodymium YAG, is the sum of the neodymium
atoms that are isolated in

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:33.060
the glass that contains yttrium and aluminium
together to form so-called

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.970
the g is for garnet, which describes the structure
of the solid, and if you

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:43.340
excite this the neodymium atoms can emit light
in the infrared, like heat

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.159
rays you can’t see them. And then further
up here there is another

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:53.759
crystal which turns this infrared light into
green light so the green light

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:55.039
comes out at the end.

00:01:55.039 --> 00:02:00.469
And also when neodymium is combined with iron
and boron it can form a

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:03.710
type of magnet which we call a ‘Permanent
Magnet’ and it is extremely

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:06.579
strong, one of the strongest that we actually
know of.

00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:11.340
In order to get this doubling from the infrared
to the green to work, you

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.709
need to have pulses of light. So green lasers,
although to the eye, look

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:21.560
as if they are continuous are actually very
rapid pulses which are very

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:27.540
intense which is why these pointers are quite
dangerous if you point

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.370
them in the wrong direction in a lecture.
Because I wave my hands so

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:38.739
much, I never use these in lectures. But I
know that you the cameraman

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:40.690
have also used…

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.000
Hello.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.870
…used neodymium, because neodymium is element
number 60 and the

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.780
cameraman’s future mother-in-law was 60
a few days ago and so we

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.720
provided a sample of neodymium, element 60,
for him to present to her

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.519
as a birthday present.

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:01.520
Happy Birthday Marion!

